        ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $Z$ nor to $N.$ We can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\notin N\\qquad 3.25\\notin Z\n$$\n\n- However, it can be written as the ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n3.25=\\frac{325}{100}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\in Q\n$$\n\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $R$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\in R\\qquad 3.25\\in C\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $Q, R, C$ only."
